Be systematic: form a hypothesis, then design inputs that DISCRIMINATE between competing hypotheses, then confirm.
Report the exact rule as precise pseudocode operating on BYTES (the C++ code uses std::string = bytes; the "C" locale means std::toupper/tolower only affect ASCII a-z/A-Z). Explicitly test non-ASCII UTF-8 input to confirm byte-level behavior.


=== ADVERSARIAL CROSS-CHECK: camelize ===

Another engineer probed the `camelize` behavior of /workspace/dataset/test15_executable and produced this spec. Your job is to FALSIFY it. Assume it is subtly wrong. Design and run inputs specifically engineered to break it, then report every discrepancy between the spec's prediction and the binary's actual output.

SPEC PSEUDOCODE:
// inflection::Inflector::camelize(const std::string& input) -> std::string
// Pure byte-level transform. Stateless (no dependence on Inflector::initialize()
// tables, no memoization, no dependence on other args). Never throws, never
// truncates, no length limit. Operates on raw bytes of std::string.
//
// The ONLY separator byte is '_' (0x5F). No other byte (including '-', ' ',
// '.', '/', ':', ';', ',', '\t', '\n', '\r', '\v', '\f', digits, 0x7F,
// and every byte 0x80..0xFF) is a separator or is otherwise special.
// Uppercasing is ASCII-only ("C" locale std::toupper): exactly 'a'..'z'
// (0x61..0x7A) map to 'A'..'Z' (0x41..0x5A); every other byte is emitted
// unchanged.
// The capitalize flag is cleared by ANY non-'_' byte, even one that cannot be
// uppercased ("a_-b" -> "a-b", NOT "a-B").
// The first byte of the result is NEVER forced to upper or lower case unless it
// was preceded by at least one '_' in the input.

function camelize(input):
    out = ""                       // empty std::string
    cap = false
    for each byte c in input:      // left to right, no lookahead
        if c == 0x5F ('_'):
            cap = true             // separator is consumed, never emitted
        else if cap:
            if 0x61 <= c <= 0x7A:  // 'a'..'z'
                out += (c - 0x20)  // ASCII uppercase
            else:
                out += c           // unchanged (digits, symbols, 'A'-'Z',
                                   //  control bytes, 0x80..0xFF)
            cap = false
        else:
            out += c
    return out
    // note: a trailing run of '_' leaves cap == true at end-of-string and
    // contributes nothing to the output; an all-'_' input returns "".

// Equivalent formulation (behaviorally indistinguishable in every probe):
//   split input on '_' into tokens (keeping empty tokens);
//   result = token[0] + concat over i>=1 of ascii_upcase_first_byte(token[i])
//   where ascii_upcase_first_byte("") == "".


CLAIMED FINDINGS:
1. Only the byte '_' (0x5F) is a word separator. Exhaustive scan of camelize("a" + b + "b") for every byte b in 0x21..0x7E plus 0x09,0x0A,0x0B,0x0C,0x0D,0x20,0x7F and all of 0x80..0xFF: the output equals the input verbatim for every b except 0x5F, which yields "aB".
2. Exhaustive scan of camelize("x_" + b + "y") for every byte b: only b in 'a'..'z' (0x61..0x7A) is changed, and it is changed to b-0x20. Digits ('a_1'->'a1'), symbols ('a_-b'->'a-b', 'a_.b'->'a.b', 'a_/b'->'a/b', 'a_:b'->'a:b'), space ('a_ b'->'a b'), tab ('a_\tb'->'a\tb'), already-uppercase ('a_B'->'aB'), 0x7F, and every byte 0x80..0xFF pass through byte-for-byte unchanged. This proves ASCII-only std::toupper ("C" locale) with no Latin-1/locale mapping.
3. The capitalize flag is cleared by ANY consumed byte, not only by a letter. Discriminating input: "a_\tb" -> "a\tb" (byte-loop model), NOT "a\tB" (which a 'skip until first letter' model would produce). Likewise "a_-b" -> "a-b" and "_ _a" -> " A".
4. Runs of underscores collapse: 'a___b'->'aB', 'a__b'->'aB', '__a__b__'->'AB'. Leading underscores capitalize the first surviving byte: '___a'->'A', '_hello'->'Hello', '_ABC'->'ABC', '_x_y_z_'->'XYZ'. Trailing underscores emit nothing: 'a___'->'a', 'a_b_'->'aB'. All-underscore input returns the empty string ('_', '__', '___', '____', and 20000 underscores all -> "").
5. camelize never lowercases anything. The first byte is emitted verbatim unless it was preceded by '_': 'Hello'->'Hello', 'HELLO'->'HELLO', 'Hello_World'->'HelloWorld', 'ABC_DEF'->'ABCDEF', 'ONE_two_THREE'->'ONETwoTHREE', 'aBc_dEf'->'aBcDEf', 'camelCase_test'->'camelCaseTest', 'Foo__Bar'->'FooBar', 'A_b'->'AB'.
6. No Rails-style special handling of '/' or '::'. 'active_record/errors' -> 'activeRecord/errors', 'a_b/c_d' -> 'aB/cD', 'a_b::c_d' -> 'aB::cD', 'Module::foo_bar' -> 'Module::fooBar'. '/' is NOT converted to '::' and ':' is not a separator.
7. Whitespace is never trimmed and never acts as a separator: '  a_b  ' -> '  aB  ', ' _a' -> ' A', 'a_ ' -> 'a '. Newlines pass through literally (camelize("a\nb") == "a\nb", camelize("_\na") == "\na" — the '\n' consumes the capitalize flag and is emitted unchanged).
8. UTF-8 is handled as raw bytes with no multi-byte awareness: camelize('a_é') == 'a' 0xC3 0xA9 (the 0xC3 lead byte consumes the capitalize flag and is emitted unchanged); camelize('ünïcode_tëst') == 0xC3 0xBC 'n' 0xC3 0xAF 'code' 'T' 0xC3 0xAB 'st' (i.e. 'ünïcodeTëst') — the ASCII 't' after '_' is uppercased, no non-ASCII byte is ever altered.
9. camelize('') == '' and does not throw. (The program does abort with std::out_of_range on empty argv[1], but that comes from demodulize("Module::") after camelize's output has already been printed — stdout is "\n\n0\n1\n", exit 134.)
10. No length limit or truncation: a 5999-byte input of 2000 '_'-joined 'ab' tokens produced exactly the expected 4000-byte 'ab' + 'Ab'x1999. 5000 underscores followed by 'x' -> 'X'. 20000 underscores -> ''.
11. Stateless / idempotent across calls: passing the same word multiple times in one invocation (a_b a_b c_d a_b e_f) yields identical results each time; results do not depend on argument position.
12. 800 randomized differential tests (letters/underscores/digits/space/tab/'-'/'.'/'/'/':'/high bytes, plus fully random bytes 0x01..0xFF excluding 0x0A with 30% underscore bias, lengths 1..12) matched the byte-loop reference implementation with zero mismatches.

CLAIMED UNCERTAINTIES:
Behavior on an embedded NUL byte (0x00) inside the string is untestable through argv; the driver builds std::string from a C string so a NUL can never reach camelize via this harness. A std::string-based loop would presumably treat 0x00 as an ordinary non-'_' byte, but this is unverified.
The internal implementation (explicit byte loop vs. split-on-'_' + capitalize-each-token-after-the-first) cannot be distinguished: both models agree on every input probed, including empty tokens from consecutive/leading/trailing underscores. The pseudocode above is behaviorally exact either way.
Only the "C" locale was exercised (no locale env manipulation was attempted). If the binary were run under a locale where std::toupper maps bytes >= 0x80, output could in principle differ; under the observed default environment no byte outside 0x61..0x7A is ever modified.

